\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


A real-valued function $u\in C^2(\mathbb R^3)$ is harmonic on all of $\mathbb R^3$ if
\[\Delta u=\frac{\partial^2u}{\partial x_1^2}+\frac{\partial^2u}{\partial x_2^2}+\frac{\partial^2u}{\partial x_3^2}=0.
\]
For $p>0$, let $C_p=\{x\in\mathbb R^3:x_1^2+x_2^2=px_3^2\}$, including both sheets and the vertex.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Determine all $p>0$ for which there is a nonzero harmonic function $u$ on the whole of $\mathbb R^3$ such that $u(x)=0$ for every $x\in C_p$. \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Which circular cones are contained in the zero set of a nontrivial harmonic function defined everywhere in three-dimensional space?

